  "reason": "Frontend and pipeline medians are lower than the unchanged native C control on all six frozen inputs, but process CPU, live RSS and fresh-child wall losses remain. Every frontend, pipeline and child-wall median remains above both historical Canonical and Dense controls. This does not establish the required no-regression result or justify an official frontend/syntax cutover.",
  "scope": "Frozen flattened whole-program inputs, old Canonical/Dense controls, current flat TS control and compiled A0 frontend. Parser/checker plus one historical C text emitter. Frontend instantiation/upload/typed-IR decoding included. Graph I/O, shipping CLI startup, formatter, model inference and generated-target execution excluded. Every fresh process preloads the common candidate SDK and its selected artifact; candidate artifact also preloaded for historical and TS controls. Per-sample preload records identify every Wasm component, hash and size. Artifact validation and Wasm module compilation are outside frontend/pipeline phases; fresh-child wall time includes process startup and preparation. No official source cutover or no-regression conclusion is implied.",
  "limitations": [
    "Single machine and one frozen rotated run; no confidence interval or significance conclusion.",
    "Generated target execution, shipping CLI startup, graph loading, model editing/inference and total AI computation were not measured.",
    "Fresh-process wall includes the selected artifact preload and engine preparation. Smaller artifact size alone does not establish faster compilation or lower total cost.",
    "Process frontend CPU includes all process threads in the phase; it is not a function self-time.",
    "Initial peak RSS has a shared launch/preload floor on smaller inputs; live frontend memory and all raw fields are retained.",
    "No C projection, read-array optimizer effect or combined source algorithm is attributed to this isolated comparison.",
    "Independent correctness evidence 2dace596 is separate from the six frozen whole-program output checks in this measurement."
